# The warden on the barrier. Gate B of chapter one.
#
# The rule here is the mirror of the clerk's, and the pair is the point: the
# clerk would not release a thing because it was on a list, and this one will not
# release a person because he is not on one. In both cases what wins is a
# document about an *object* — the same delivery label, used twice for two
# different purposes. That is the chapter's thesis in two rooms: paperwork about
# things outranks paperwork about people.
#
# Requires has:documents rather than a flag, so it is genuinely gated on Lino
# having the box with him and not merely on having solved gate A. Carrying it is
# the point; he cannot leave it behind.

uid    baggadlgward01
colour 0.86 0.78 0.52

line start
    says DLG_WARDEN_START

    option DLG_WARDEN_OPT_WHY
        if    !asked_why
        raise asked_why
        reply DLG_WARDEN_REPLY_WHY

    option DLG_WARDEN_OPT_RESIDENT
        if    !asked_resident
        raise asked_resident
        reply DLG_WARDEN_REPLY_RESIDENT

    # The wrong answer, and it stays available: it is where the distinction the
    # puzzle turns on gets stated, and the player may need it twice.
    option DLG_WARDEN_OPT_ID
        if    asked_resident !passed_checkpoint
        reply DLG_WARDEN_REPLY_ID

    option DLG_WARDEN_OPT_SHOW
        if   has:documents !passed_checkpoint
        goto show

    option DLG_WARDEN_OPT_AGAIN
        if    passed_checkpoint
        reply DLG_WARDEN_REPLY_AGAIN

    option DLG_WARDEN_OPT_BYE
        reply DLG_WARDEN_REPLY_BYE
        end

# He objects that it is three years old, which is true and which the rule does
# not mention. The player says so, and that is the whole of gate B.
line show
    says DLG_WARDEN_SHOW

    option DLG_WARDEN_OPT_SHOW_YES
        raise passed_checkpoint
        reply DLG_WARDEN_REPLY_SHOW_YES
        end

    option DLG_WARDEN_OPT_SHOW_NO
        goto start
